  }}
  .badge-leo {{ background:#dbeafe; color:#1d4ed8; }}
  .badge-geo {{ background:#fef3c7; color:#92400e; }}
  .badge-meo {{ background:#d1fae5; color:#065f46; }}
  .badge-heo {{ background:#fee2e2; color:#991b1b; }}
  .badge-other {{ background:#f3f4f6; color:#374151; }}
  .badge-unknown {{ background:#f3f4f6; color:#9ca3af; }}

  /* ── Bar mini ── */
  .bar-bg {{
    background: var(--light);
    border-radius: 4px;
    height: 8px;
    width: 100%;
  }}
  .bar-fill {{
    background: var(--blue);
    border-radius: 4px;
    height: 100%;
  }}

  /* ── Callout ── */
  .callout {{
    border-left: 4px solid var(--blue);
    background: #eff6ff;
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    margin: 20px 0;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    color: var(--dark-blue);
  }}
  .callout.amber {{
    border-left-color: var(--accent);
    background: #fffbeb;
    color: #78350f;
  }}
  .callout.green {{
    border-left-color: var(--green);
    background: #ecfdf5;
    color: #064e3b;
  }}
  .callout strong {{ font-weight: 700; }}

  /* ── Stats grid ── */
  .stats-grid {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 16px 0;
  }}
  .stat-cell {{
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 14px 12px;
    text-align: center;
  }}
  .stat-val {{
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--blue);
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }}
  .stat-lbl {{
    font-size: 10.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.5px;
    color: var(--gray);
    font-weight: 600;
  }}

  /* ── Two-col text ── */
  .two-col {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 32px;
    margin: 16px 0;
  }}

  /* ── Footer ── */
  footer {{
    background: var(--dark-blue);
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.5);
    text-align: center;
    padding: 24px;
    font-size: 11px;
    margin-top: 48px;
  }}
  footer b {{ color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); }}

  /* ── TOC ── */
  .toc {{
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 24px 28px;
    margin: 32px 0 0;
    display: inline-block;
    min-width: 320px;
  }}
  .toc-title {{
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
    color: var(--gray);
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }}
  .toc ol {{
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 18px;
    font-size: 13px;
  }}
  .toc li {{ margin-bottom: 6px; }}
  .toc a {{ color: var(--blue); text-decoration: none; }}
  .toc a:hover {{ text-decoration: underline; }}

  @media (max-width: 760px) {{
    .kpi-banner {{ grid-template-columns: repeat(2,1fr); }}
    .chart-grid {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr; }}
    .stats-grid {{ grid-template-columns: repeat(3,1fr); }}
    .two-col {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr; }}
    .cover {{ padding: 40px 24px 32px; }}
    .container {{ padding: 0 16px; }}
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- ══════════════════════ COVER ══════════════════════ -->
<div class="cover">
  <div class="cover-tag">Orbital Analytics Report · April 2026</div>
  <h1>Active Satellites in Earth Orbit<br>2026 Catalog Analysis</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">
    A data-driven examination of the {k['total']:,} active satellites tracked by CelesTrak/NORAD,
    covering orbital regime distribution, constellation composition, launch trends,
    and key orbital parameters — produced for space-industry analysts and policy advisors.
  </p>
  <div class="cover-meta">
    <span><b>Dataset:</b> CelesTrak / NORAD TLE Catalog</span>
    <span><b>EPOCH Range:</b> {epoch_range}</span>
    <span><b>Rows:</b> {k['total']:,} active satellites</span>
    <span><b>Published:</b> April 2026</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════════ KPI BANNER ══════════════════════ -->
<div class="kpi-banner">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{k['total']:,}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Active Satellites</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">As of EPOCH range</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value accent">{k['leo_share']}%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">LEO Share</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">{k['leo_count']:,} satellites · MM &gt; 14 rev/day</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value red">{k['starlink']:,}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Starlink Fleet Size</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">{k['starlink_pct']}% of total fleet</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value green">{k['median_inc']}°</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Median Inclination</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Degrees above equatorial plane</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{k['post2020']:,}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Launched Since 2020</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">{k['post2020_pct']}% of active fleet</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════════ MAIN BODY ══════════════════════ -->
<div class="container">

  <!-- TOC -->
  <div class="toc">
    <div class="toc-title">Contents</div>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#overview">Catalog Overview &amp; Key Figures</a></li>
      <li><a href="#orbit-regime">Orbital Regime Classification</a></li>
      <li><a href="#constellation">Major Constellation &amp; Operator Breakdown</a></li>
      <li><a href="#launch-trends">Launch Year Trends</a></li>
      <li><a href="#orbital-char">Orbital Parameter Deep Dive</a></li>
      <li><a href="#conclusions">Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 1 — OVERVIEW ═══ -->
  <div class="section" id="overview">
    <div class="section-num">SECTION 01</div>
    <h2>Catalog Overview &amp; Key Figures</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      The CelesTrak/NORAD active-satellite catalog captures {k['total']:,} operational spacecraft as of
      {epoch_range}. This snapshot encompasses the full spectrum of Earth-orbital missions —
      from mega-constellations to singular scientific probes — and forms the authoritative basis
      for all figures in this report.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Data coverage:</strong> Every metric, chart, and percentage in this report is computed
      directly from the {k['total']:,}-row dataset. No values have been imputed or externally sourced.
      Orbital classification uses MEAN_MOTION thresholds as described in Section 2.
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h3>Fleet Composition at a Glance</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--gray); font-size:13px; margin-bottom:14px;">
          LEO dominates the fleet at <strong>{k['leo_share']}%</strong> of all active satellites,
          driven overwhelmingly by Starlink. GEO hosts the traditional communications and weather
          satellite fleet. MEO carries navigation constellations (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, BeiDou).
          HEO represents a small minority of highly elliptical surveillance and communication missions.
        </p>
        <div class="callout amber">
          <strong>Starlink effect:</strong> {k['starlink']:,} of {k['total']:,} active satellites
          ({k['starlink_pct']}%) belong to SpaceX's Starlink mega-constellation. This single operator
          skews every aggregate statistic — inclination, mean motion, LEO share — dramatically toward
          its chosen shell parameters (~53° inclination, ~15 rev/day). All subsequent analysis
          segments results with this context in mind.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>EPOCH &amp; Data Freshness</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--gray); font-size:13px; margin-bottom:14px;">
          The EPOCH field records when each orbital element set was last updated. The full range
          spans <strong>{epoch_range}</strong>, indicating that the vast majority of TLE records
          are current within a narrow recent window — consistent with the CelesTrak practice of
          refreshing elements daily for active payloads.
        </p>
        <h3>Mean Motion &amp; Inclination Summary</h3>
        <div class="stats-grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);">
          <div class="stat-cell">
            <div class="stat-val">{mm_stats['mean']:.2f}</div>
            <div class="stat-lbl">Mean · Rev/Day</div>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-cell">
            <div class="stat-val">{mm_stats['median']:.2f}</div>
            <div class="stat-lbl">Median · Rev/Day</div>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-cell">
            <div class="stat-val">{inc_stats['Mean']:.1f}°</div>
            <div class="stat-lbl">Mean Inclination</div>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-cell">
            <div class="stat-val">{inc_stats['Median']:.1f}°</div>
            <div class="stat-lbl">Median Inclination</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 2 — ORBIT REGIME ═══ -->
  <div class="section" id="orbit-regime">
    <div class="section-num">SECTION 02</div>
    <h2>Orbital Regime Classification</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Satellites are classified into four regimes using MEAN_MOTION (orbital revolutions per day)
      and ECCENTRICITY thresholds derived from the TLE catalog. The thresholds applied are:
    </p>

    <div class="callout green">
      <strong>Classification thresholds:</strong>
      <strong>LEO</strong> = MEAN_MOTION &gt; 14.0 rev/day ·
      <strong>GEO</strong> = 0.9 ≤ MEAN_MOTION ≤ 1.1 rev/day ·
      <strong>MEO</strong> = 2.0 &lt; MEAN_MOTION ≤ 14.0 rev/day (non-GEO, non-HEO) ·
      <strong>HEO</strong> = ECCENTRICITY &gt; 0.25 and MEAN_MOTION ≤ 2.0 rev/day
    </div>

    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-card">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['orbit']}" alt="Orbital Regime Donut Chart"/>
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 1 — Distribution of {k['total']:,} active satellites by orbital regime</p>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; justify-content:center;">
        <div class="tbl-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Regime</th><th>Count</th><th>Share</th><th>Description</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>{orbit_rows()}</tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--gray); margin-top:12px;">
          LEO's dominance ({k['leo_share']}%) is a direct consequence of mega-constellation
          deployment. Without Starlink alone, LEO's share would fall to approximately
          {round((k['leo_count'] - k['starlink']) / (k['total'] - k['starlink']) * 100, 1)}% —
          still the largest regime, but far less skewed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 3 — CONSTELLATION ═══ -->
  <div class="section" id="constellation">
    <div class="section-num">SECTION 03</div>
    <h2>Major Constellation &amp; Operator Breakdown</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Constellation membership is inferred from OBJECT_NAME prefixes (e.g., "STARLINK-", "ONEWEB-",
      "GPS ", "GLONASS-"). Satellites not matching a known prefix are grouped as
      "Other / Independent."
    </p>

    <div class="callout amber">
      <strong>Starlink dominance:</strong> SpaceX's Starlink constellation accounts for
      <strong>{k['starlink']:,} satellites ({k['starlink_pct']}% of the entire active fleet)</strong>.
      This single constellation exceeds the combined count of every other identified operator
      in the catalog by a substantial margin, reshaping spectrum allocation pressures,
      orbital debris risk profiles, and conjunctions in the 500–600 km LEO shell.
    </div>

    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-card" style="grid-column: 1 / -1;">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['const']}" alt="Top Constellations Bar Chart"/>
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 2 — Top 15 constellations/operators by active satellite count</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Constellation Detail Table</h3>
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr>
          <th>Constellation / Operator</th>
          <th>Active Satellites</th>
          <th>Fleet Share (%)</th>
          <th>Relative Scale</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>{const_rows()}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--gray); margin-top:10px;">
      Satellites identified by prefix matching. "Other / Independent" aggregates all remaining
      satellites that do not match a known constellation pattern.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 4 — LAUNCH TRENDS ═══ -->
  <div class="section" id="launch-trends">
    <div class="section-num">SECTION 04</div>
    <h2>Launch Year Trends</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Launch year is extracted from the first four characters of OBJECT_ID
      (format: <code>YYYY-NNNXXX</code>). This analysis counts currently <em>active</em> satellites
      by the year they were launched — representing fleet survival rather than total historical
      launch volume.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Post-2020 explosion:</strong> <strong>{k['post2020']:,} satellites ({k['post2020_pct']}%)</strong>
      of the entire active fleet were launched in 2020 or later — a stunning compression of orbital
      history. The active fleet effectively doubled, then doubled again, within a five-year window
      driven almost entirely by Starlink v1/v2 deployments.
    </div>

    <div class="chart-grid single">
      <div class="chart-card">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['launch']}" alt="Launch Year Trend Chart"/>
        <p class="chart-caption">
          Figure 3 — Annual launches (still active, bars) and cumulative active fleet (red line)
          by launch year. Gold bars = post-2020. Dashed line marks the 2020 acceleration boundary.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h3>Post-2020 Annual Detail</h3>
        <div class="tbl-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Launch Year</th><th>Active Satellites</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{post2020_rows()}</tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decade Summary</h3>
        <div class="tbl-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Decade</th><th>Active Satellites</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {"".join(f"<tr><td>{int(r['Decade'])}s</td><td class='num'>{int(r['Active Satellites']):,}</td></tr>" for r in decade_table if r['Decade'] and str(r['Decade']) != 'nan')}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--gray); margin-top:10px;">
          Satellites from earlier decades remain active due to the longevity of MEO navigation
          satellites (GPS, GLONASS) and GEO communications platforms with multi-decade design lives.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 5 — ORBITAL CHARACTERISTICS ═══ -->
  <div class="section" id="orbital-char">
    <div class="section-num">SECTION 05</div>
    <h2>Orbital Parameter Deep Dive</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Three parameters — INCLINATION, ECCENTRICITY, and MEAN_MOTION — characterize where and
      how each satellite orbits. Their distributions reveal the dominant orbital shells and
      flag operational outliers.
    </p>

    <!-- Inclination -->
    <h3>Inclination Distribution</h3>
    <div class="chart-grid single">
      <div class="chart-card">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['inc']}" alt="Inclination Distribution"/>
        <p class="chart-caption">
          Figure 4 — Inclination histogram (2° bins). Amber highlight = Starlink ~53° cluster;
          green highlight = polar/SSO ~90°. Red dashed line = median ({k['median_inc']}°).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="stats-grid">
      <div class="stat-cell">
        <div class="stat-val">{inc_stats['Min']}°</div>
        <div class="stat-lbl">Minimum</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-cell">
        <div class="stat-val">{inc_stats['Max']}°</div>
        <div class="stat-lbl">Maximum</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-cell">
        <div class="stat-val">{inc_stats['Mean']}°</div>
        <div class="stat-lbl">Mean</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-cell">
        <div class="stat-val">{inc_stats['Median']}°</div>
        <div class="stat-lbl">Median</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-cell">
        <div class="stat-val">{inc_stats['Std Dev']}°</div>
        <div class="stat-lbl">Std Dev</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      The dominant spike near <strong>53°</strong> is Starlink's primary shell inclination.
      A secondary cluster near <strong>90°</strong> represents polar and sun-synchronous orbit (SSO)
      missions — remote sensing, meteorology, and science payloads. GEO satellites cluster near
      <strong>0°</strong> (equatorial), though with slight inclination drift in aging platforms.
    </div>

    <!-- Eccentricity -->
    <h3>Eccentricity Classification</h3>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-card">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['ecc']}" alt="Eccentricity Classification"/>
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 5 — Eccentricity grouped into four operational bands</p>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; justify-content:center; gap:12px; padding:20px 0;">
        <div class="callout green" style="margin:0;">
          <strong>{ecc_stats['near_circular']:,}</strong> satellites
          ({round(ecc_stats['near_circular']/k['total']*100,1)}%) have near-circular orbits
          (ecc &lt; 0.01), consistent with station-keeping by operational constellations.
        </div>
        <div class="callout" style="margin:0;">
          <strong>{ecc_stats['low']:,}</strong> ({round(ecc_stats['low']/k['total']*100,1)}%)
          show low eccentricity (0.01–0.1), typical of aging LEO payloads and transfer orbit
          remnants still catalogued as active.
        </div>
        <div class="callout amber" style="margin:0;">
          <strong>{ecc_stats['high']:,}</strong> satellites (ecc ≥ 0.3) are in highly elliptical
          orbits — likely HEO missions designed for extended apogee dwell over specific
          latitudes (e.g., Molniya-type coverage of high northern latitudes).
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Mean Motion -->
    <h3>Mean Motion Distribution</h3>
    <div class="chart-grid single">
      <div class="chart-card">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['mm']}" alt="Mean Motion Distribution"/>
        <p class="chart-caption">
          Figure 6 — Mean motion histogram. Amber dashed = LEO threshold (14 rev/day);
          green dashed = GEO signature (~1.0 rev/day).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="stats-grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);">
      <div class="stat-cell">
        <div class="stat-val">{mm_stats['min']:.2f}</div>
        <div class="stat-lbl">Min Rev/Day</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-cell">
        <div class="stat-val">{mm_stats['max']:.2f}</div>
        <div class="stat-lbl">Max Rev/Day</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-cell">
        <div class="stat-val">{mm_stats['mean']:.2f}</div>
        <div class="stat-lbl">Mean Rev/Day</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-cell">
        <div class="stat-val">{mm_stats['median']:.2f}</div>
        <div class="stat-lbl">Median Rev/Day</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ SECTION 6 — CONCLUSIONS ═══ -->
  <div class="section" id="conclusions">
    <div class="section-num">SECTION 06</div>
    <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      The following findings synthesize the data analysis into actionable observations for
      spectrum policy, orbital debris governance, and constellation licensing.
    </p>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h3>1 — LEO Crowding Is a Single-Operator Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--gray);">
          {k['leo_share']}% of active satellites operate in LEO, and {k['starlink_pct']}% of all
          satellites are Starlink alone. The conjunctions, spectrum coordination challenges, and
          debris mitigation requirements in the 500–600 km shell are, to an unusual degree,
          concentrated around a single operator's decisions. Licensing frameworks should address
          operator-level concentration, not just aggregate orbital-regime population limits.
        </p>

        <h3>2 — The Post-2020 Fleet Is Unprecedented</h3>
        <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--gray);">
          {k['post2020_pct']}% of the active fleet was launched after 2019. No historical orbital
          precedent exists for this rate of change. Deorbit planning, frequency coordination, and
          interference modeling all depend on stable assumptions about fleet size — assumptions
          that no longer hold. Regulators should build adaptive review cycles into constellation
          licenses rather than fixed-term grants.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>3 — Inclination Clusters Signal Interference Choke Points</h3>
        <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--gray);">
          The extreme concentration of satellites near 53° inclination (Starlink primary shell)
          creates dense interference zones for ground stations operating in that latitude band.
          Secondary cluster near 90° (SSO) similarly concentrates RF traffic at polar latitudes.
          Spectrum coordination should model inclination density, not just orbital altitude.
        </p>

        <h3>4 — Navigation Constellation Stability</h3>
        <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--gray);">
          GPS, GLONASS, BeiDou, and Galileo maintain stable MEO populations. These constellations
          have predictable replacement cycles and stable inclinations — providing a fixed reference
          against which LEO dynamics should be measured. Their orbital shells remain largely
          uncrowded by commercial entrants, though O3b MEO broadband activity warrants monitoring.
        </p>

        <h3>5 — Near-Circular Orbits Confirm Active Station-Keeping</h3>
        <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--gray);">
          {round(ecc_stats['near_circular']/k['total']*100,1)}% of satellites maintain
          near-circular orbits (ecc &lt; 0.01), indicating active ion or chemical propulsion.
          This is relevant for debris planning: propulsive satellites can deorbit on command,
          while the {round(ecc_stats['high']/k['total']*100,1)}% in high-eccentricity HEO
          orbits present longer atmospheric residence times and require case-by-case end-of-life
          planning.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout amber" style="margin-top:24px;">
      <strong>Bottom line for policy:</strong> The 2026 orbital environment is not a gradual
      evolution of the 2015 environment — it is a structural discontinuity driven by a single
      commercial actor's mega-constellation. Any spectrum allocation, debris liability, or
      orbital slot governance framework that does not explicitly account for mega-constellation
      dynamics will be structurally inadequate within the licensing window of any satellite
      approved today.
    </div>
  </div>

</div><!-- /container -->

<footer>
  <b>Active Satellites in Earth Orbit — 2026 Catalog Analysis</b><br/>
  Data source: CelesTrak / NORAD Two-Line Element (TLE) catalog via Kaggle
  (ibrahimqasimi/active-satellites-currently-in-orbit) ·
  {k['total']:,} records · EPOCH range: {epoch_range} ·
  All figures computed directly from source data · No imputed values
</footer>

</body>
</html>"""

